ERROR CENSUS AND THE PAIR COMPARISON
  error-or-severe-severity-lines-this-side: 2
  distinct-error-severity-shapes-this-side: 2
  severe-severity-lines-this-side: 0
  shapes-present-on-both-sides: 2
  shapes-present-only-after-the-change-set: 0
  shapes-present-only-before-the-change-set: 0
  shapes-undisposed: 0 of 2

  At the signature level, which is broader than the severity level because it also
  matches exception and stack-frame lines carrying no severity marker of their own:
  signature-shapes-present-only-after-the-change-set: 4
  signature-shapes-present-only-before-the-change-set: 6
  the post-migration-only signature shapes, listed rather than counted, each
  with whatever the ledger says about it:
    <TIMESTAMP> [INFO ] [main] org.springframework.security.config.http.HttpSecurityBeanDefinitionParser - Checking sorted filter chain: [Root bean: class [org.springframework.security.web.session.Disable <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: informational-not-an-error
      evidence: An INFO line in which the security framework lists the filter chain it just assembled. It reaches this census only because one member of that list is named ExceptionTranslationFilter, so the word Exception appears inside a component name; the line itself reports success. Its text differs between the two sides because the change set advances the security library within its generation - AAP 0.5.1 records the advance and its compatibility reason, and 0.1.2 records that the generation itself is held - and the newer line adds DisableEncodeUrlFilter to the chain while the older one names SecurityContextPersistenceFilter. A chain composition that differs is the expected consequence of that advance and is not an error on either side. Verifiable in the region itself: the same lines appear at INFO severity in spring-context.log and in catalina.out.log on both sides, and neither side carries an error-severity line from this component.
    <TIMESTAMP> [INFO ] [main] org.springframework.security.web.DefaultSecurityFilterChain - Will secure Regex [pattern='^/api/.*$|^/plugin/.*$'] with [org.springframework.security.web.session.DisableEnco <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: informational-not-an-error
      evidence: An INFO line reporting one assembled filter chain: which request pattern it covers and, when the chain is non-empty, which filters it applies.  It reaches this census only because the enumerated filter list names ExceptionTranslationFilter.  The two sides differ in WORDING and not in behaviour, and that was measured rather than assumed over the archived windows: both sides assemble exactly 21 chains, their 21 request patterns are a byte-identical set, exactly 18 of the 21 carry an EMPTY filter list on both sides, and the same 3 patterns carry filters on both sides - the interface routes, the static-asset routes, and the catch-all.  The newer security library reports an empty chain as "Will not secure <pattern>" where the older one reported it as "Will secure <pattern> with []", which is why the count of lines matching the word "secure" differs (3 versus 21) while the chain set does not.  Re-derivable from either side's catalina.out.log: extract every DefaultSecurityFilterChain line, strip the "Will secure" or "Will not secure" prefix and the filter list, sort, and the two sides produce the same 21 lines.
    <TIMESTAMP> [INFO ] [main] org.springframework.security.web.DefaultSecurityFilterChain - Will secure any request with [org.springframework.security.web.session.DisableEncodeUrlFilter@<HASH>, org.sprin <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: informational-not-an-error
      evidence: An INFO line reporting one assembled filter chain: which request pattern it covers and, when the chain is non-empty, which filters it applies.  It reaches this census only because the enumerated filter list names ExceptionTranslationFilter.  The two sides differ in WORDING and not in behaviour, and that was measured rather than assumed over the archived windows: both sides assemble exactly 21 chains, their 21 request patterns are a byte-identical set, exactly 18 of the 21 carry an EMPTY filter list on both sides, and the same 3 patterns carry filters on both sides - the interface routes, the static-asset routes, and the catch-all.  The newer security library reports an empty chain as "Will not secure <pattern>" where the older one reported it as "Will secure <pattern> with []", which is why the count of lines matching the word "secure" differs (3 versus 21) while the chain set does not.  Re-derivable from either side's catalina.out.log: extract every DefaultSecurityFilterChain line, strip the "Will secure" or "Will not secure" prefix and the filter list, sort, and the two sides produce the same 21 lines.
    <TIMESTAMP> [WARN ] [main] org.springframework.security.config.http.DefaultFilterChainValidator - Possible error: Filters at position 8 and 9 are both instances of org.springframework.security.web.aut <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: informational-not-an-error
      evidence: A WARN line the security framework emits when two adjacent filters in a chain are instances of the same authentication filter class. It is pre-existing behaviour of this application's chain configuration and appears on BOTH sides; only the two position numbers it quotes differ, because the newer library inserts an additional filter earlier in the same chain and shifts the pair along. The condition it warns about is unchanged, and it is a warning rather than an error on either side.
  the pre-migration-only signature shapes, listed rather than counted, each
  with whatever the ledger says about it:
    <TIMESTAMP> [DEBUG] [Exec Stream Pumper] com.armedia.acm.userinterface.angular.AngularResourceCopier - >> Error: Node Sass does not yet support your current environment: Linux 64-bit with Unsupported  <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: removed-by-migration
      evidence: Emitted by the pre-migration stylesheet compiler's own installer as it fails to find a prebuilt binary for this platform. Same family as the sass.js task-loading row below: the change set removes that toolchain outright, so the line cannot occur after it. AAP 0.5.2 records the removal and 0.6.6 records the measurement that makes it output-neutral. Present only on the pre-migration side, which the gate treats as a removal rather than a regression.
    <TIMESTAMP> [DEBUG] [Exec Stream Pumper] com.armedia.acm.userinterface.angular.AngularResourceCopier - Loading "sass.js" tasks...ERROR
      classification: removed-by-migration
      evidence: The pre-migration frontend toolchain declares a stylesheet-compiler task plugin whose native compiler cannot build on a current runtime, and its loader reports the failure as it starts. The change set removes that plugin outright — AAP 0.5.2 records the removal and 0.6.6 records why removal rather than replacement is output-neutral: the build file declares no task for it, and the asset resolver consumes only compiled stylesheet globs. The shape therefore appears only on the pre-migration side. It is a migration IMPROVEMENT, not a regression, and the gate treats a pre-migration-only shape accordingly.
    <TIMESTAMP> [INFO ] [main] org.springframework.security.config.http.HttpSecurityBeanDefinitionParser - Checking sorted filter chain: [Root bean: class [org.springframework.security.web.context.Securit <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
