    <TIMESTAMP> [INFO ] [main] org.springframework.security.web.DefaultSecurityFilterChain - Will secure any request with [org.springframework.security.web.context.SecurityContextPersistenceFilter@<HASH>, <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: informational-not-an-error
      evidence: An INFO line reporting one assembled filter chain: which request pattern it covers and, when the chain is non-empty, which filters it applies.  It reaches this census only because the enumerated filter list names ExceptionTranslationFilter.  The two sides differ in WORDING and not in behaviour, and that was measured rather than assumed over the archived windows: both sides assemble exactly 21 chains, their 21 request patterns are a byte-identical set, exactly 18 of the 21 carry an EMPTY filter list on both sides, and the same 3 patterns carry filters on both sides - the interface routes, the static-asset routes, and the catch-all.  The newer security library reports an empty chain as "Will not secure <pattern>" where the older one reported it as "Will secure <pattern> with []", which is why the count of lines matching the word "secure" differs (3 versus 21) while the chain set does not.  Re-derivable from either side's catalina.out.log: extract every DefaultSecurityFilterChain line, strip the "Will secure" or "Will not secure" prefix and the filter list, sort, and the two sides produce the same 21 lines.
    <TIMESTAMP> [WARN ] [main] org.springframework.security.config.http.DefaultFilterChainValidator - Possible error: Filters at position 6 and 7 are both instances of org.springframework.security.web.aut <TRUNCATED-AT-200>
      classification: informational-not-an-error
      evidence: A WARN line the security framework emits when two adjacent filters in a chain are instances of the same authentication filter class. It is pre-existing behaviour of this application's chain configuration and appears on BOTH sides; only the two position numbers it quotes differ, because the newer library inserts an additional filter earlier in the same chain and shifts the pair along. The condition it warns about is unchanged, and it is a warning rather than an error on either side.
  A signature-only difference does not fail this gate and is not silently dropped
  either: it is listed above so a reader can see what it is.  The gate turns on
  SEVERITY, because a line the application chose to log at error severity is the
  thing AAP 0.9.4 speaks about, while an exception name inside an informational
  line is not.

  WHY THE COMPARISON IS OVER SHAPES AND NOT OVER COUNTS.  Several of these shapes
  come from scheduled jobs that fire on a fixed cadence, so how many times one
  appears is a function of how long the window happened to be rather than of
  anything the change set did.  Two windows of different lengths would therefore
  differ in multiplicity while describing identical behaviour, and a count-based
  comparison would report that as a regression.  Per-side occurrence counts are
  still published, per shape, in error-scan.txt beside this file — they are
  informative and they are simply not what the gate turns on.

PROCESS-DEFINITION TOTAL, OBSERVED
  The review recorded this figure as unobserved.  It is observed here from the
  engine's own log, inside the archived region, so it is re-derivable by a reader
  with no database and no running stack:
  scan-patterns-the-deployer-reported: classpath*:/activiti/acm*.bpmn20.xml,classpath*:/activiti/ArkCase*.bpmn20.xml,classpath*:/activiti/DefaultComplaint*.bpmn20.xml,classpath*:/activiti/Corrective*.bpmn20.xml,classpath*:/activiti/Media*.bpmn20.xml
  resources-the-application-deployer-found: 8
  resources-the-engine-deployer-processed-into-definitions: 8
  observed-on-the-other-side: 8

  RECONCILIATION TO THE TRACKED SET, DERIVED RATHER THAN ASSERTED.  The pattern
  list above is parsed out of the deployer's own line and matched against the
  repository's tracked main-source resources, so the expected set comes from what
  the application said it looked for rather than from what a person believed:
  expected-set-basis: parsed from the deployer's own logged pattern list
  tracked-main-source-resources-the-patterns-select: 8
    acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-complaint-plugin/src/main/resources/activiti/DefaultComplaintWorkflow_v5.bpmn20.xml
    acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-task-plugin/src/main/resources/activiti/ArkCase Buckslip Process v4.bpmn20.xml
    acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-task-plugin/src/main/resources/activiti/Corrective Action Plan_v3.bpmn20.xml
    acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-task-plugin/src/main/resources/activiti/Corrective Task Action Plan.bpmn20.xml
    acm-services/acm-service-ecm/src/main/resources/activiti/acmDocumentSingleTaskWorkflow_v2.bpmn20.xml
    acm-services/acm-service-ecm/src/main/resources/activiti/acmDocumentTaskWorkflow_v1.bpmn20.xml
    acm-services/acm-service-ecm/src/main/resources/activiti/acmDocumentWorkflow_v5.bpmn20.xml
    acm-services/acm-service-media-engine/src/main/resources/activiti/MediaEngineWorkFlow_v2.bpmn20.xml
  A tracked resource that matches a pattern but lies under a TEST resource root is
  correctly absent from the deployed set and from the count above, because the
  classpath scan runs against the packaged application rather than the repository.
  Resources outside these patterns belong to the extension applications that
  deploy their own processes and are not part of this deployment's total.

THE OTHER TWO CONDITIONS AAP 0.9.4 NAMES, OBSERVED
  The packaged application and the three frontend files the change set creates:
  packaged-application: arkcase-2021.03.war
  three-new-frontend-files: PRESENT — all three are inside arkcase-2021.03.war, which holds 2575 entries
  The three are the committed lockfile, the runtime version pin and the generated
  configuration module.  No packaging descriptor change was needed for them, because
  no project file in this reactor declares a packaging exclusion or a web-resource
  override, so their absence would indicate a genuine packaging fault rather than a
  missing exclusion edit.  UNAVAILABLE is not a failure and is not counted as one: a
  build output is deleted by the next clean build, whereas the regions this record is
  otherwise computed from remain re-derivable from the archive indefinitely.

  The aspect auto-proxy configuration:
  aspect-classes-in-tracked-main-source: 7
  tracked-main-resource-files-enabling-aspect-auto-proxying: 5
  root-context-reached-full-initialisation: migrated yes, pre-migration yes
  WHAT DISCHARGES THIS CONDITION, AND WHAT DOES NOT.  The framework does not log
  advisor or proxy creation at informational severity, and this deployment logs the
  framework at informational severity, so there is no per-aspect line to point at and
  saying otherwise would be an invention.  What IS observed is stronger than a count
  of log lines: the aspect beans and the auto-proxy post-processor are members of the
  root context, and a failure to build an advisor or to proxy a target aborts the
  context refresh rather than degrading quietly.  The context reaching full
  initialisation on BOTH runtimes is therefore the observation, and the two counts
  above state how large the surface is that it covered.  This is the practical
  confirmation of the migration analysis that left the aspect library unchanged.

WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT
  It is not a behavioural verdict on the eight flows; those carry their own
  per-flow records and their own comparison.  It is not a claim that the
  environment is complete: a service the reference stack does not provide shows up
  as an error shape present on BOTH sides, which is exactly how it should read.
  And it is never an exit status of a grep — see error-scan.txt beside it.
